sentences.t:
“Merced vos pidimos commo a rey e a señor;
con vuestro conssejo lo queremos fer nos,
que nos demandedes fijas del Campeador;
casar queremos con ellas a su ondra y a nuestra pro.”

sentences.l:
“Mercy of-you we-ask as to king and to lord;
with your counsel it we-want to-do we,
that for-us you-ask-for daughters of-the Campeador;
to-marry we-want with them to his honour and to our profit.”

sentences.t:
Fabló Minaya e plogo a Per Vermu[doz]:
“Rogar gelo emos lo que dezides vos;
después faga el Çid lo que oviere sabor.”

sentences.l:
Spoke Minaya and it-pleased to Per Vermu[doz]:
“To-ask to-him-it we-will what you say;
afterward let-do the Çid what he-may-have liking.”

sentences.t:
Quando lo sopo el buen Campeador,
apriessa cavalga, a reçebirlos salió;
sonrrisós mio Çid e bien los abraçó:

sentences.l:
When it knew the good Campeador,
in-haste he-rides, to receive-them he-went-out;
smiled-himself my Çid and well them embraced:

sentences.t:
Dixo el Çid: “de grand natura son ifantes de Carrión,
ellos son mucho urgullosos e an part en la cort,
deste çasamiento non avría sabor;

sentences.l:
Said the Çid: “of great lineage are infantes of Carrión,
they are much proud and they-have part in the court,
of-this marriage not I-would-have liking;
